      "content": "146|### The Jar\n147|\n148|A black ceramic vessel rests upon a stone pedestal.\n149|\n150|Ghostly faces occasionally push outward from its surface before disappearing.\n151|\n152|Each jar contains several tortured souls.\n153|\n154|Touching it causes the creature to hear dozens of overlapping whispers.\n155|\n156|[[Skarguther Dungleboof]] offers **300 gp for each intact jar** delivered to him, up to **1,200 gp** for the four active jars in the ritual chamber. He insists that every vessel remain fully intact and undamaged so that the tortured souls remain preserved inside the relic.\n157|\n158|This creates a clear moral choice. The characters can carry the jars to Skarguther and claim the payment, knowingly consigning the trapped souls to a collector of cursed and unholy relics. Or they can smash the jars, which releases the souls and allows them to pass into the afterlife, but destroys the vessels and forfeits Skarguther’s reward. Characters who choose both may release some souls while keeping other jars intact, but Skarguther pays only for undamaged vessels.\n159|\n160|### Breaking the Jar\n161|\n162|AC 12  ",
