      "hasMoreBelow": true,
      "nextStartLine": 171,
      "content": "150|Characters who accepted [[Skarguther Dungleboof]]’s [[The Roach Clip]] contract can identify this as the item he wants. He pays **100 gp for each intact inert jar** in Area 3 and **300 gp for each intact active jar** in the ritual chamber. The vessel must remain sealed and undamaged; a broken jar earns nothing. Characters who did not accept the contract can still recognize that the jar would interest collectors of forbidden magic, but selling it would be dangerous and morally fraught.\n151|\n152|This creates a clear choice. The characters can carry the jar to Skarguther, placing its tortured souls in the hands of a Duster who claims he can guide them toward [[Heralds of Dust|True Death]]. Or they can smash it, freeing the souls and allowing them to pass into the afterlife, but destroying the vessel and forfeiting its reward. They may choose differently for each jar, but Skarguther pays only for intact vessels.\n153|\n154|### Breaking the Jar\n155|\n156|AC 12  \n157|HP 10  \n158|Immune: Poison, Psychic\n159|\n160|Destroying the jar releases the souls trapped inside. A violent wave of spectral energy fills the chamber. Every creature within 10 feet must make a **DC 12 Wisdom saving throw**, taking **2d6 Psychic damage** on a failed save, or half as much damage on a successful one. A creature that fails the save is also **Frightened for 1 minute**. The creature can repeat the saving throw at the end of each of its turns, ending the effect on itself on a success.\n161|\n162|Outside combat, the effect lasts long enough to matter: a frightened character may flee the chamber, drop what they are carrying, or be unable to approach another jar until they recover. The spirits then disappear. A character proficient in Arcana or Religion realizes that the souls have finally been allowed to pass into the afterlife.\n163|\n164|### Taking the Jar\n165|\n166|The characters may instead carry the intact vessel. It is heavy, fragile, and constantly whispers to anyone holding it. Wrapping it securely prevents accidental damage, but does not silence the voices.\n167|\n168|If the characters accepted Skarguther’s contract, they must transport the jar to the Dungsweepers’ mortuary offices in [[The Undercity Osslurraggath]] and present it to Skarguther or one of his Duster attendants to collect the reward. Otherwise, carrying it could attract the attention of necromancers, undead creatures, religious authorities, or [[Dopendis Gravebreath]].\n169|\n170|---",
